fflush(NULL) and fflush_unlocked(NULL) flush only the streams with
pending writes, as glibc and musl do. They flushed every stream, and a
flush discards what a stream has read ahead: on a file it seeks back
first, so nothing is lost, but a pipe cannot give the bytes again.
Programs flush every stream before they fork or run popen(), and
PostgreSQL's single-user server, which initdb feeds its setup through a
pipe, runs `locale -a` with popen() while it reads that input: it lost
the rest of its buffer and read a statement from the middle of a
comment. Meant for upstream.

